WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.251
This briefing comes from
Book 03 of the Federal

2
00:00:03.251 --> 00:00:06.287
Modernization Workbook,
HR-Driven Identity.

3
00:00:06.287 --> 00:00:09.930
It has one proposal, and I'll
take it through four questions,

4
00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:13.877
the way I'd take it to leadership:
why it matters, what we should do,

5
00:00:13.877 --> 00:00:17.975
who has to be involved, and how we'd
carry it out and know it's working.

6
00:00:17.975 --> 00:00:23.287
Book 02 made the case that identity is
now where every access decision is made.

7
00:00:23.287 --> 00:00:27.025
This book asks where the facts behind
that identity should come from.

8
00:00:27.025 --> 00:00:30.760
The answer is the HR system
of record, starting now.

9
00:00:31.510 --> 00:00:32.160
Why.

10
00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:36.160
In many agencies today, nothing
connects HR to identity.

11
00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:40.679
Accounts, access and credentials
are created and removed by ticket,

12
00:00:40.679 --> 00:00:42.373
and that costs three ways.

13
00:00:42.373 --> 00:00:44.209
Access outlives the job.

14
00:00:44.209 --> 00:00:48.348
When someone leaves or changes
roles, removal waits for a ticket.

15
00:00:48.348 --> 00:00:53.050
Yet the federal PIV standard, FIPS
201-3, requires a card that can't

16
00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:58.197
be collected to be terminated within
eighteen hours of notification.

17
00:00:58.197 --> 00:01:01.136
A ticket-driven process
struggles to meet that.

18
00:01:01.136 --> 00:01:04.095
Records drift: the phone
book, the manager and the

19
00:01:04.095 --> 00:01:07.573
approval routing are wrong
until someone files a ticket.

20
00:01:07.573 --> 00:01:11.134
And every joiner, mover and
leaver is help-desk work.

21
00:01:11.134 --> 00:01:13.585
Zero Trust makes the gap visible.

22
00:01:13.585 --> 00:01:16.373
A token proves
cryptographically who signed it.

23
00:01:16.373 --> 00:01:20.148
It can't prove the claim is true:
that the person still works here,

24
00:01:20.148 --> 00:01:23.269
still holds the position
the access was granted for,

25
00:01:23.269 --> 00:01:26.173
still reports to the
supervisor who approved it.

26
00:01:26.173 --> 00:01:29.197
Those are workforce facts,
and they live in HR.

27
00:01:29.197 --> 00:01:34.135
If we do nothing, HR moves to Core
HCM, identity moves to Entra ID,

28
00:01:34.135 --> 00:01:39.440
and we rebuild the same manual
process twice, on new platforms.

29
00:01:40.190 --> 00:01:40.977
Why now?

30
00:01:40.977 --> 00:01:44.190
Because two migrations
open one design window.

31
00:01:44.190 --> 00:01:51.232
On the HR side: the December 10, 2025 memo
from OMB and OPM creates Federal HR 2.0,

32
00:01:51.232 --> 00:01:55.815
one government-wide core
human capital platform.

33
00:01:55.815 --> 00:02:00.091
OPM awarded the platform
contract on June 10, 2026,

34
00:02:00.091 --> 00:02:05.515
and the first agencies begin
moving as fiscal year 2027 starts.

35
00:02:05.515 --> 00:02:09.176
The memo's sample timeline runs
about eight months per agency,

36
00:02:09.176 --> 00:02:13.052
with the agency's interconnections
going live around month five.

37
00:02:13.052 --> 00:02:16.578
On the identity side:
agencies are moving from

38
00:02:16.578 --> 00:02:20.566
Active Directory to Entra ID
anyway, which is Book 02.

39
00:02:20.566 --> 00:02:25.477
In Entra ID, attributes drive Conditional
Access, groups and delegation,

40
00:02:25.477 --> 00:02:30.553
and inbound provisioning from an HR
system is a documented, supported path.

41
00:02:30.553 --> 00:02:31.703
So there's a window.

42
00:02:31.703 --> 00:02:35.206
The identity feed has to be
designed in during onboarding,

43
00:02:35.206 --> 00:02:39.002
before the interconnection goes
live, not bolted on afterward.

44
00:02:39.002 --> 00:02:44.327
One honest limit: Federal HR 2.0
covers federal employees only.

45
00:02:44.327 --> 00:02:48.043
Contractors and vendors need
their own authoritative source,

46
00:02:48.043 --> 00:02:51.480
and the onboarding is the
right time to decide it too.

47
00:02:52.230 --> 00:02:53.230
What should we do?

48
00:02:53.230 --> 00:02:58.552
There are four realistic options, weighed
against what leadership has to balance:

49
00:02:58.552 --> 00:03:02.230
the mission and the public,
security, cost and resilience.

50
00:03:02.230 --> 00:03:06.430
Compliance matters, but on its
own it isn't a reason to invest.

51
00:03:06.430 --> 00:03:09.030
Option A keeps ticket-driven identity.

52
00:03:09.030 --> 00:03:11.805
No new investment, but
the risk and the manual

53
00:03:11.805 --> 00:03:14.880
work stay where they are:
the cost of doing nothing.

54
00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:19.405
Option B automates inside the
directories with scripts and exports.

55
00:03:19.405 --> 00:03:23.944
It's quick and cheap, but it automates
a source that isn't authoritative,

56
00:03:23.944 --> 00:03:29.030
so it spreads bad data faster, and it has
to be rebuilt when HR changes platforms.

57
00:03:29.030 --> 00:03:33.392
Option C waits for HR 2.0
and integrates afterward.

58
00:03:33.392 --> 00:03:36.092
That's defensible, and
it's less work this year.

59
00:03:36.092 --> 00:03:39.457
But the interconnection decisions
are made during onboarding,

60
00:03:39.457 --> 00:03:41.806
so waiting means
retrofitting after go-live.

61
00:03:41.806 --> 00:03:46.996
Option D starts now: HR as the source
of truth, expressed in OPM's standard

62
00:03:46.996 --> 00:03:53.067
personnel data and designed into both the
HR onboarding and the Entra ID migration.

63
00:03:53.067 --> 00:03:56.834
It costs design effort up front
and needs shared governance,

64
00:03:56.834 --> 00:03:59.093
which is why it's only partial on cost.

65
00:03:59.093 --> 00:04:04.567
The judgment call: if HR 2.0 slipped
a long way, C becomes reasonable.

66
00:04:04.567 --> 00:04:07.037
But D's first step, data standards,

67
00:04:07.037 --> 00:04:11.320
ownership and the person key,
is no-regret either way.

68
00:04:12.070 --> 00:04:15.470
What does "HR as the source
of truth" actually mean?

69
00:04:15.470 --> 00:04:17.832
It means one owner for every fact.

70
00:04:17.832 --> 00:04:22.032
The HR system of record is
authoritative for employment status,

71
00:04:22.032 --> 00:04:25.757
position, organization,
supervisor and effective dates.

72
00:04:25.757 --> 00:04:29.525
The identity platform stays
authoritative for what it does:

73
00:04:29.525 --> 00:04:33.895
credentials, authentication, sessions,
directory objects and policy.

74
00:04:33.895 --> 00:04:36.875
And contractors, whom
HR 2.0 doesn't cover,

75
00:04:36.875 --> 00:04:41.570
get their own authoritative source
mapped into the same model.

76
00:04:41.570 --> 00:04:44.670
That model is the canonical
workforce identity model.

77
00:04:44.670 --> 00:04:49.011
Its contract is OPM's standard data
elements and nature-of-action codes,

78
00:04:49.011 --> 00:04:51.881
the government-wide
personnel data standards,

79
00:04:51.881 --> 00:04:55.633
so vendor field names stay at
the boundary and can be replaced.

80
00:04:55.633 --> 00:05:00.437
It's keyed on an enterprise person key
the agency controls, and that key has

81
00:05:00.437 --> 00:05:05.557
to fit Entra ID's employeeId attribute,
which holds at most sixteen characters.

82
00:05:05.557 --> 00:05:09.248
The rule that makes it work:
every attribute gets one name,

83
00:05:09.248 --> 00:05:11.996
one definition and one accountable owner.

84
00:05:11.996 --> 00:05:17.220
When a fact is wrong, it gets fixed in its
source, never patched in the directory.

85
00:05:17.970 --> 00:05:22.020
Provisioning turns personnel
records into governed identities.

86
00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:26.483
Core HCM publishes worker
records and personnel actions.

87
00:05:26.483 --> 00:05:30.558
A small middleware, inside the
agency's authorization boundary,

88
00:05:30.558 --> 00:05:35.712
does five things for every event: it
validates the record, applies events in order,

89
00:05:35.712 --> 00:05:38.096
normalizes them to the SCIM standard,

90
00:05:38.096 --> 00:05:41.557
writes a provenance record,
and reconciles the result.

91
00:05:41.557 --> 00:05:46.275
Then Entra ID's API-driven inbound
provisioning creates and updates the

92
00:05:46.275 --> 00:05:51.307
accounts in Entra ID and, through the
provisioning agent, in Active Directory.

93
00:05:51.307 --> 00:05:55.076
That interface has daily call
limits that scale with the license,

94
00:05:55.076 --> 00:05:58.146
which the book sizes against
the agency's population.

95
00:05:58.146 --> 00:05:59.970
The design fails closed.

96
00:05:59.970 --> 00:06:02.020
Unknown worker types are refused.

97
00:06:02.020 --> 00:06:06.233
A failed record is retried and
recorded, never silently skipped.

98
00:06:06.233 --> 00:06:09.950
And every event leaves a provenance
record before the call returns,

99
00:06:09.950 --> 00:06:12.407
which is where the
evidence comes from later.

100
00:06:12.407 --> 00:06:15.394
The technology is the
example, not the point.

101
00:06:15.394 --> 00:06:19.395
Today it's Oracle Core
HCM feeding Entra ID.

102
00:06:19.395 --> 00:06:24.940
If the vendors change, the model and the
rules hold; only the boundary changes.

103
00:06:25.690 --> 00:06:27.015
Who has to be involved.

104
00:06:27.015 --> 00:06:30.678
This is an organizational change
as much as a technical one.

105
00:06:30.678 --> 00:06:31.527
Owners.

106
00:06:31.527 --> 00:06:35.528
HR owns the personnel data and
its quality, at the source.

107
00:06:35.528 --> 00:06:41.865
The OPM HR 2.0 program owns the platform,
the standards and the interconnection.

108
00:06:41.865 --> 00:06:44.515
And each contractor source
owns its own record.

109
00:06:44.515 --> 00:06:45.415
Users.

110
00:06:45.415 --> 00:06:51.490
The CIO's identity team runs Entra ID,
Active Directory and access from that data.

111
00:06:51.490 --> 00:06:55.765
Security sets the Zero Trust
policy and owns the leaver path.

112
00:06:55.765 --> 00:06:59.802
And the help desk moves from routine
tickets to handling exceptions.

113
00:06:59.802 --> 00:07:00.615
Guards.

114
00:07:00.615 --> 00:07:03.115
The privacy office is in from the start.

115
00:07:03.115 --> 00:07:08.292
Sharing HR records with identity inside
the agency normally rests on the Privacy

116
00:07:08.292 --> 00:07:10.043
Act's need-to-know provision,

117
00:07:10.043 --> 00:07:13.622
while disclosures outside the
agency need a routine use,

118
00:07:13.622 --> 00:07:16.895
so the privacy office decides
the basis for each leg,

119
00:07:16.895 --> 00:07:20.702
along with the privacy impact
assessment and minimization.

120
00:07:20.702 --> 00:07:23.865
The PIV office owns
issuance and termination.

121
00:07:23.865 --> 00:07:29.790
And the ISSO and authorizing official
own the evidence and any accepted risk.

122
00:07:29.790 --> 00:07:33.300
The data architect ties it
together: owning the standards,

123
00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:38.272
the crosswalk and the person key, making
sure every attribute has one accountable

124
00:07:38.272 --> 00:07:43.320
owner, and carrying the standard to the
interagency forums where it's adopted.

125
00:07:44.070 --> 00:07:44.683
How.

126
00:07:44.683 --> 00:07:49.707
Personnel actions drive the three
identity events, by code, not by ticket.

127
00:07:49.707 --> 00:07:53.433
A joiner gets an account and
baseline access ready on day one.

128
00:07:53.433 --> 00:07:56.473
A mover has the old
assignment's access removed,

129
00:07:56.473 --> 00:07:59.982
the new baseline applied,
and any exceptions reviewed.

130
00:07:59.982 --> 00:08:01.889
And a leaver is blocked first:

131
00:08:01.889 --> 00:08:06.971
the account disabled and sessions
revoked at the effective separation time,

132
00:08:06.971 --> 00:08:12.133
then credentials, devices and access
removed in parallel, each one reconciled.

133
00:08:12.133 --> 00:08:15.308
The book is precise about what
the platform actually does.

134
00:08:15.308 --> 00:08:18.201
Lifecycle Workflows run on one tenant-wide

135
00:08:18.201 --> 00:08:21.496
schedule and need an Entra
ID Governance license.

136
00:08:21.496 --> 00:08:24.770
Revoking sessions stops
new tokens being issued,

137
00:08:24.770 --> 00:08:28.045
but tokens already issued
last until they expire.

138
00:08:28.045 --> 00:08:31.441
Privileged leavers need their
own verified block path,

139
00:08:31.441 --> 00:08:35.870
because the built-in disable task
doesn't support users with admin roles.

140
00:08:35.870 --> 00:08:39.256
And synced users must be
deleted in Active Directory,

141
00:08:39.256 --> 00:08:41.333
or the next sync brings them back.

142
00:08:41.333 --> 00:08:46.209
Credentials follow the same events:
PIV issuance, certificate binding and

143
00:08:46.209 --> 00:08:51.620
termination, and device certificates
retired before the account is deleted.

144
00:08:52.370 --> 00:08:53.620
Next, access.

145
00:08:53.620 --> 00:08:57.857
Once the attributes are trustworthy,
access is derived from them.

146
00:08:57.857 --> 00:09:02.566
A personnel record becomes a canonical
attribute, a rule or access package

147
00:09:02.566 --> 00:09:06.820
turns that into an entitlement, and
an evidence record captures why.

148
00:09:06.820 --> 00:09:11.190
Every entitlement then traces back
to a rule, an approved exception,

149
00:09:11.190 --> 00:09:15.170
an authoritative attribute, or
a privileged-access decision.

150
00:09:15.170 --> 00:09:17.420
That's what makes access explainable.

151
00:09:17.420 --> 00:09:20.429
Drift is found by
comparison, not by complaint:

152
00:09:20.429 --> 00:09:24.308
scheduled checks compare HR,
the model and the directories.

153
00:09:24.308 --> 00:09:28.957
It's measured in business terms
leadership can read: time from separation

154
00:09:28.957 --> 00:09:32.996
to blocked access, orphaned accounts,
and access ready on day one.

155
00:09:32.996 --> 00:09:35.320
And the records have to
be kept long enough.

156
00:09:35.320 --> 00:09:40.308
Entra ID keeps audit and provisioning
logs for thirty days with P1 or P2,

157
00:09:40.308 --> 00:09:43.520
so the book exports them
from the pilot onward.

158
00:09:43.520 --> 00:09:45.720
The evidence comes as a byproduct.

159
00:09:45.720 --> 00:09:50.220
The same records that run the lifecycle
answer the auditor's oldest question:

160
00:09:50.220 --> 00:09:54.520
does every account belong to a
current person with a current reason?

161
00:09:55.270 --> 00:10:00.745
The execution plan has seven phases, and
each gate is the next phase's precondition.

162
00:10:00.745 --> 00:10:06.732
Phase zero aligns the program, naming
identity as a consumer in both migrations.

163
00:10:06.732 --> 00:10:10.232
Phase one settles attribute
authority and the schema.

164
00:10:10.232 --> 00:10:12.732
Phase two builds and tests the pipeline.

165
00:10:12.732 --> 00:10:14.569
Phase three is the pilot.

166
00:10:14.569 --> 00:10:18.895
Phase four turns on lifecycle automation
and attribute-derived access.

167
00:10:18.895 --> 00:10:22.195
Phase five cuts over
population by population.

168
00:10:22.195 --> 00:10:23.995
And phase six sustains it.

169
00:10:23.995 --> 00:10:25.457
Four rules run through it.

170
00:10:25.457 --> 00:10:29.114
Start now, from the HR data
the agency already has,

171
00:10:29.114 --> 00:10:32.770
and switch the source to
Core HCM when it arrives.

172
00:10:32.770 --> 00:10:35.845
Run in shadow mode first,
comparing what HR says

173
00:10:35.845 --> 00:10:38.920
with what the directories
hold, and fix the gaps.

174
00:10:38.920 --> 00:10:44.308
Cut over one population at a time, with a
signed validation report and a rollback.

175
00:10:44.308 --> 00:10:47.118
And close the ticket path for a population

176
00:10:47.118 --> 00:10:50.169
only when the data proves
HR is the sole source.

177
00:10:50.169 --> 00:10:54.820
The gate thresholds, like ninety-nine
percent correlation, are proposals.

178
00:10:54.820 --> 00:10:58.106
They're starting criteria
the agency approves through

179
00:10:58.106 --> 00:11:01.107
its own risk governance,
not federal requirements.

180
00:11:01.107 --> 00:11:06.010
Chapter 13 walks an illustrative
agency through all seven phases.

181
00:11:06.760 --> 00:11:09.110
How we'll know it's
working, and what we need.

182
00:11:09.110 --> 00:11:11.718
Five measures, each
baselined in phase one,

183
00:11:11.718 --> 00:11:14.922
and each one a business
measure leadership can read.

184
00:11:14.922 --> 00:11:18.373
Time from separation to
blocked access, in hours.

185
00:11:18.373 --> 00:11:22.672
Accounts tied to a person, with
orphaned accounts trending to zero.

186
00:11:22.672 --> 00:11:26.910
Joiner and mover tickets going
down, with access ready on day one.

187
00:11:26.910 --> 00:11:30.923
The manager and organization
in the directory matching HR.

188
00:11:30.923 --> 00:11:33.722
And every exception with
an owner and an age.

189
00:11:33.722 --> 00:11:38.984
What we need from leadership is one
decision: start HR-driven identity now,

190
00:11:38.984 --> 00:11:44.247
as a design requirement of the HR 2.0
onboarding and the Entra ID migration.

191
00:11:44.247 --> 00:11:48.851
And to act on it: a sponsor across
HR, the identity team and privacy;

192
00:11:48.851 --> 00:11:54.235
identity named as a consumer in the HR
2.0 interconnection plan; data owners

193
00:11:54.235 --> 00:11:57.187
designated and the person key confirmed;

194
00:11:57.187 --> 00:12:00.661
and a small team for the
pre-onboarding data work.

195
00:12:00.661 --> 00:12:05.364
Book 03 has the full design, with
every external claim footnoted,

196
00:12:05.364 --> 00:12:09.447
and Book 02 covers the directory
migration it builds on.

197
00:12:09.447 --> 00:12:10.260
Thank you.
